  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x-5y-7).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-5y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-5y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(z-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(z-7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(z-7).$",
